'''Sudanese Kaftians of the Bolodun and Kuvari Islands''' are an ethnic group in the [[Bolodun and Kuvari Islands]]. They form the largest ethnic group in the country, comprising an estimated 57.2 percent of the population — roughly 66,000 people as of 2025 — and are concentrated on the island of [[Bolodun]]. Most Sudanese Kaftians in the islands trace their origins to [[Ayorin]] and the surrounding region of Sudanese Kaftia, in the continent of [[Kaftia]].

The group descends primarily from enslaved Kaftians brought to the islands by the [[Burawa|Burawi]] slave trade, which began in 1533. After emancipation in 1789, freed Sudanese Kaftians were settled on Bolodun under the [[Emancipation of Slaves Act (Capuyaquiran League)|Emancipation of Slaves Act]], where they established an autonomous administration under [[Iacob Kheme]]. Their majority position on Bolodun was secured through the [[Kuvari War]] (1796–1801), after which the island was administered by the Sudanese Kaftian community. A smaller population of modern Sudanese Kaftian immigrants, mainly from [[Dakare]] and [[Ayorin]], has also settled in the islands in recent decades.

== Origins ==

Sudanese Kaftians originate from Sudanese Kaftia, the region north of [[Dakare]] in the continent of [[Kaftia]]. Most families in the islands trace their ancestry to [[Ayorin]], a region of eastern Kaftia known for its rainforest and highland ecology, together with surrounding areas along the [[Great Kaftian Range]]. The identification with Ayorin is preserved in family memory and in the community's internal traditions, which distinguish the majority Ayorin-origin families from the smaller lineages drawn from elsewhere in Sudanese Kaftia.

== History ==

=== Enslavement and the plantation colony ===

The [[Burawi slave trade]], which began in 1533, brought enslaved Kaftians to Burawi's New World colonies to replace native populations diminished by disease. The Kuvari islands, incorporated into the [[Badweyn Islands]] colony and managed by the [[Lacashe]] province of [[New Burawa]], were developed as a sugar-producing plantation colony worked by enslaved labour. Enslaved Sudanese Kaftians formed the workforce of the plantations on the islands, with Bolodun emerging as the principal island of the group. In 1695 Bolodun was separated administratively from the rest of the colony and given its own governor.

=== Emancipation ===

Following the [[Abolition of Slavery Act]] in Burawa in 1789, the [[Capuyaquiran League]] banned slavery the same year. The [[Emancipation of Slaves Act (Capuyaquiran League)|Emancipation of Slaves Act]] ordered that emancipated slaves who did not sign contracts to work on existing farms be relocated to Bolodun, which was granted autonomous control under [[Iacob Kheme]]. The arrival of freed Sudanese Kaftians on Bolodun, where the [[Kuvari people|Kuvari]] population had lived under an oppressive social hierarchy, produced an era of civil unrest that escalated into the [[Kuvari War]] (1796–1801). The League provided military support to the Sudanese Kaftian administration, and the war ended with the killing of large numbers of Kuvari on Bolodun and the expulsion of the survivors to the other islands.

=== Modern era ===

After the war, Bolodun was administered by the Sudanese Kaftian community, while the Kuvari administration led from [[Tumala]]. The two administrations remained estranged for much of the following two centuries, although shared neglect and prejudice from the other League islands encouraged rapprochement. After independence in 1926, the multiracial coalition built by the [[Socialist Party of Bolodun]] drew heavily on the Sudanese Kaftian political class, and the islands' political institutions — including the presidency and the [[People's Assembly of Bolodun and Kuvari Islands|People's Assembly]] — have since included leading Sudanese Kaftian figures.

In addition to the descendants of the enslaved population, a smaller number of modern Sudanese Kaftian immigrants have settled in the islands in recent decades, coming mainly from [[Dakare]] and [[Ayorin]].

== Culture ==

Sudanese Kaftians in the islands speak [[Burawi]] as their primary language, reflecting both their origin within the Burawi colonial sphere and their long history on the islands. The community is predominantly [[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]]. Family and communal identity centres on Bolodun, where the majority of the population lives, and the memory of the emancipation era and the Kuvari War remains central to the community's sense of itself.

